<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    THE SEVEN THAT WERE GIVEN  —  the bow line
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    THE DIRECTION, and it is a third register again. The Three Gods are
    instruments of a founding act, cosmic and unowned. The recovered five
    are inheritances, named by a guild clerk after whatever habit got the
    last owner killed. These are neither: every one of them was GIVEN.

    The accounts are all the same shape. Somebody was standing somewhere
    they had no business surviving, something put a bow in their hands,
    and nobody was asked whether they wanted it. So the guild files these
    apart from the recovered weapons - a recovered weapon has a previous
    OWNER, and these have a previous BEARER, which is a different
    relationship and a worse one. You do not pick one of these up. It is
    handed to you, and the difficulty has always been handing it back.

    The Harp God is the exception that makes the frame legible: it is the
    only one that was never given to anyone. Every other file in the vault
    names a bearer. That one names the people who put it down again.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    THE YARDSTICK, and why it had to be worked out rather than assumed.

    The melee line had an obvious reference: a persona monosword. RANGED
    HAS NO PERSONA WEAPONS AT ALL. Odyssey ships a "unique" line that
    looks like the equivalent and is not - CompProperties_UniqueWeapon
    rolls traits onto UNCHANGED base stats, so a unique greatbow is a
    greatbow. Measured against the actual files, the vanilla ranged
    ceiling is:

        charge rifle    8.13 effective DPS   (14.12 raw)
        minigun         9.81 effective DPS   (41.67 raw, terrible accuracy)
        unique greatbow 3.64 effective DPS

    "Effective" is raw DPS weighted across the range bands a firefight
    actually happens in (15% touch, 40% short, 35% medium, 10% long),
    because a sniper rifle and a minigun are not comparable on raw damage.

    These seven are deliberately parked ABOVE that, at the melee line's
    own numbers - 2.3x a charge rifle at S rank and 3.4x at Nation. That
    is a decision, not an accident: it makes a bow from a gate strictly
    better than anything a colony can build or buy, which is the point of
    an artifact, and it means these should stay gate rewards rather than
    ever entering a trader pool as ordinary stock.

        Snow Flake      19.1    Butterfly Heart 22.2    Nebula Star  25.0
        Lavander Rose   18.9    Sea Will        22.1    Unicorn Star 25.0
                                                        Harp God     28.0

    Each still owns exactly ONE axis and gives ground on the others, the
    same rule the recovered five follow, so the pairs inside a rank are
    not one bow printed twice.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    QUALITY WORKS DIFFERENTLY HERE, and it caught me out. Melee quality
    runs through MeleeWeapon_DamageMultiplier, where Good is already
    1.10. Ranged runs through RangedWeapon_DamageMultiplier and
    RangedWeapon_ArmorPenetrationMultiplier, whose curve is FLAT:

        awful 0.9   poor 1.0   normal 1.0   good 1.0
        excellent 1.0   masterwork 1.25   legendary 1.5

    Two consequences worth writing down. First, the damage numbers below
    ARE the published figures - there is no 1.10 divisor the way there is
    in the melee files, and copying that pattern here would have quietly
    underpowered the whole line by ten percent. Second, ACCURACY IS NOT
    AFFECTED BY QUALITY AT ALL - AccuracyTouch and its siblings carry no
    StatPart_Quality, and VerbProperties.AdjustedAccuracy reads them
    straight off the equipment - so a legendary bow shoots no straighter
    than a normal one, only harder.

    That makes CompArtifactQuality nearly inert here: it floors the roll
    at Good, and Good is worth exactly what Normal is. It stays on anyway,
    for three reasons - it blocks the one roll that DOES cost damage
    (awful, 0.9), it keeps market value and hit points where an artifact
    should be, and it means the whole artifact line answers to one comp
    instead of these seven quietly being the exception.

    A legendary roll therefore multiplies damage and AP by 1.5, which puts
    the Harp God at 42 effective DPS and Snow Flake at 28.6.
    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    drawSize matches the TEXTURE CANVAS aspect, not the inked shape.
    RimWorld stretches the whole file, so an aspect taken off the ink
    bounding box distorts every one of these by the width of its own
    padding. Heights climb slightly by rank so the Harp God reads as the
    biggest thing on the field.

    No equippedAngleOffset, matching vanilla bows: these are drawn with
    the limbs vertical and the arrow leaving to the right, which is
    already the orientation RimWorld expects of a ranged weapon.
  -->

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- ===== [S] Snow Flake — the quick one ===== -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseWeapon">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_SnowFlake</defName>
    <label>[S-Rank] Snow Flake</label>
    <description>Two limbs of clear ice that have never once wept, joined by a grip somebody wrapped in ordinary leather — the only part of it that has ever needed replacing. It looses twice before a drawn breath is finished, and neither arrow is heavy. Against a padded coat that is more than enough. Against plate it is a great deal of very fast nothing, and every bearer learns the difference in a single engagement.\n\nIt was given on a mountain, to someone who had stopped walking. The account does not say what did the giving, only that it was already snowing indoors by then.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Weapons/Snow_Flake</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(0.55,1.50)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <techLevel>Spacer</techLevel>
    <ignoreIllegalLabelCharacterConfigError>true</ignoreIllegalLabelCharacterConfigError>
    <uiIconScale>1.05</uiIconScale>
    <soundInteract>Bow_Small</soundInteract>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>5400</MarketValue>
      <MaxHitPoints>200</MaxHitPoints>
      <Mass>1.2</Mass>
      <Flammability>0.1</Flammability>
      <DeteriorationRate>0.9</DeteriorationRate>
      <!-- THE SHORTEST REACH OF THE SEVEN, and the best up close. This is
           the bow you want when something is already inside the wire. -->
      <AccuracyTouch>0.90</AccuracyTouch>
      <AccuracyShort>0.96</AccuracyShort>
      <AccuracyMedium>0.85</AccuracyMedium>
      <AccuracyLong>0.62</AccuracyLong>
      <RangedWeapon_Cooldown>0.90</RangedWeapon_Cooldown>
    </statBases>
    <weaponClasses><li>Ranged</li><li>RangedLight</li><li>ShortShots</li></weaponClasses>
    <weaponTags><li>IsekaiDungeonArtifact</li></weaponTags>
    <tradeability>Sellable</tradeability>   <!-- gate rewards only: players may sell, traders never stock -->
    <thingCategories><li>WeaponsRanged</li></thingCategories>
    <!-- Light enough to run with. Its cost is written into the projectile:
         0.30 AP is the lowest in the line and ice does not punch plate. -->
    <equippedStatOffsets>
      <MoveSpeed>0.25</MoveSpeed>
      <MeleeDodgeChance>0.10</MeleeDodgeChance>
    </equippedStatOffsets>
    <comps>
      <!-- CompQuality FIRST, the Good floor SECOND. Both react to the trader
           generation hook and comps are walked in def order, so the floor has
           to be the one that runs last or a trader roll wipes it. -->
      <li><compClass>CompQuality</compClass></li>
      <li><compClass>IsekaiDungeons.CompArtifactQuality</compClass></li>
    </comps>
    <verbs>
      <li>
        <verbClass>Verb_Shoot</verbClass>
        <hasStandardCommand>true</hasStandardCommand>
        <defaultProjectile>IsekaiDungeons_Arrow_SnowFlake</defaultProjectile>
        <warmupTime>0.90</warmupTime>
        <burstShotCount>2</burstShotCount>
        <ticksBetweenBurstShots>8</ticksBetweenBurstShots>
        <range>26.9</range>
        <soundCast>Bow_Small</soundCast>
      </li>
    </verbs>
    <tools>
      <li><label>limb</label><capacities><li>Blunt</li><li>Poke</li></capacities><power>12</power><cooldownTime>1.8</cooldownTime><armorPenetration>0.12</armorPenetration></li>
    </tools>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- ===== [S] Lavander Rose — the patient one ===== -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseWeapon">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_LavanderRose</defName>
    <label>[S-Rank] Lavander Rose</label>
    <description>A crescent of pale gold closed around a stem of violet wood that is still, unmistakably, growing. There are thorns on the underside of the grip; they come back if you file them off. It draws slowly and it draws hard, and it puts one heavy shaft through things at a distance where the target has not yet decided to be worried.\n\nIts bearers are remembered as patient people. The register is careful to note that most of them were not, beforehand.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Weapons/Lavander_Rose</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(0.63,1.50)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <techLevel>Spacer</techLevel>
    <ignoreIllegalLabelCharacterConfigError>true</ignoreIllegalLabelCharacterConfigError>
    <uiIconScale>1.05</uiIconScale>
    <soundInteract>Bow_Large</soundInteract>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>5600</MarketValue>
      <MaxHitPoints>260</MaxHitPoints>
      <Mass>2.6</Mass>
      <Flammability>0.3</Flammability>
      <DeteriorationRate>0.7</DeteriorationRate>
      <!-- The mirror of Snow Flake: bad in your face, excellent at reach. -->
      <AccuracyTouch>0.68</AccuracyTouch>
      <AccuracyShort>0.90</AccuracyShort>
      <AccuracyMedium>0.94</AccuracyMedium>
      <AccuracyLong>0.86</AccuracyLong>
      <RangedWeapon_Cooldown>1.00</RangedWeapon_Cooldown>
    </statBases>
    <weaponClasses><li>Ranged</li><li>RangedHeavy</li><li>LongShots</li></weaponClasses>
    <weaponTags><li>IsekaiDungeonArtifact</li></weaponTags>
    <tradeability>Sellable</tradeability>   <!-- gate rewards only: players may sell, traders never stock -->
    <thingCategories><li>WeaponsRanged</li></thingCategories>
    <!-- Steadies the hand and slows the feet. A 56-damage shaft is the
         biggest single hit at S rank in either line, melee included. -->
    <equippedStatOffsets>
      <ShootingAccuracyPawn>0.15</ShootingAccuracyPawn>
      <MoveSpeed>-0.12</MoveSpeed>
    </equippedStatOffsets>
    <comps>
      <li><compClass>CompQuality</compClass></li>
      <li><compClass>IsekaiDungeons.CompArtifactQuality</compClass></li>
    </comps>
    <verbs>
      <li>
        <verbClass>Verb_Shoot</verbClass>
        <hasStandardCommand>true</hasStandardCommand>
        <defaultProjectile>IsekaiDungeons_Arrow_LavanderRose</defaultProjectile>
        <warmupTime>1.60</warmupTime>
        <range>33.9</range>
        <soundCast>Bow_Large</soundCast>
      </li>
    </verbs>
    <tools>
      <li><label>limb</label><capacities><li>Blunt</li><li>Poke</li></capacities><power>16</power><cooldownTime>2.0</cooldownTime><armorPenetration>0.15</armorPenetration></li>
    </tools>
  </ThingDef>

